    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>Revivalists taught that conversion was not only assent to correct doctrine but a new birth experienced in the heart, and that assurance of salvation was a normal expectation of the Christian life.</li><li>The revivals divided churches into pro-revival New Lights and anti-revival Old Lights, causing 98 schisms among <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-england">New England</a> Congregationalists, while Baptist and Methodist churches grew.</li><li>The historian Jon Butler has argued that the term Great Awakening is vague and exaggerated, because the revivals were regional and their effects minimal, while others see a larger impact.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>In 1743 James Davenport ordered his followers at New London to burn wigs, cloaks, rings and other vanities, and also books by religious authors such as John Flavel and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/increase-mather">Increase Mather</a>; he later published a retraction.</p></aside>
    </section>

